WEBVTT

00:00.490 --> 00:06.130
So when we talk about radicals, which we also call roots or sometimes certs, we want to think about

00:06.130 --> 00:08.600
them as the opposite of exponents.

00:08.620 --> 00:11.890
So for instance, here, take this radical.

00:11.890 --> 00:14.740
We read this as the square root of x.

00:14.740 --> 00:19.860
So this root sign means the square root of whatever is beneath it.

00:19.870 --> 00:22.290
So this is the square root of x.

00:22.300 --> 00:28.810
If we talk about the parts of this expression, this sign here is the radical sign or the root sign,

00:29.080 --> 00:33.510
and the value underneath the radical sign is called the radical hand.

00:33.520 --> 00:36.880
So whatever is beneath the radical sign is the radical hand.

00:36.880 --> 00:39.040
So this is the square root of x.

00:39.040 --> 00:44.110
And like we said, a radical expresses the opposite operation of an exponent.

00:44.260 --> 00:52.030
So to think about how radicals are opposite of exponents, think about the expression x times x so we

00:52.030 --> 00:54.100
have x multiplied by x.

00:54.310 --> 01:01.180
If we're using exponents to simplify this, then we would rewrite this expression as X to the power

01:01.180 --> 01:02.800
of two or x squared.

01:03.010 --> 01:10.390
So by writing this exponent here of two or this power of two, it tells us to multiply the base here

01:10.390 --> 01:12.250
x by itself two times.

01:12.250 --> 01:19.750
So having this exponent of two says Take the base x and multiply it by itself two times so x times x.

01:19.870 --> 01:23.830
But taking the square root of something asks the opposite question.

01:23.830 --> 01:29.770
It asks, What do I have to multiply by itself in order to get that base value?

01:29.770 --> 01:33.430
So in other words, if I take this base here of X.

01:34.150 --> 01:37.210
And I take the square root of that base x.

01:37.240 --> 01:41.380
Then this is asking me what do I have to multiply by itself two times?

01:41.380 --> 01:43.060
In other words, what are these components?

01:43.060 --> 01:48.130
One and two here they're going to be equal, but what are these two things that when multiplied together,

01:48.130 --> 01:50.350
are going to give me this X value?

01:50.350 --> 01:53.940
So that feels a little abstract, but think about a simple example.

01:53.950 --> 02:00.280
So if we take the square root of four, what do we have to multiply by itself to get four?

02:00.280 --> 02:07.330
Well, that's equal to two because two multiplied by two is equal to four.

02:07.330 --> 02:12.190
So notice here the similarity of this expression to this one up here.

02:12.220 --> 02:17.980
We're saying if we multiply two by itself two times, like we multiplied X by itself two times here,

02:17.980 --> 02:20.980
we're going to get four or we're going to get x squared up here.

02:21.190 --> 02:25.570
The square root operation works in the opposite direction or works backwards.

02:25.570 --> 02:30.640
It starts with the four and asks you to figure out that the square root of four is two.

02:30.670 --> 02:35.320
That two is the value we have to multiply by itself in order to get four.

02:35.320 --> 02:40.630
So in the same way the square root of nine is equal to three.

02:40.630 --> 02:46.540
Or the reason this is true is because three times three is equal to nine.

02:46.750 --> 02:52.600
Keep in mind also that this radical sign here means the square root of whatever's beneath it.

02:52.600 --> 02:55.780
But the square root is not the only kind of root we can take.

02:55.780 --> 03:01.090
We can also take cube root, fourth roots, fifth roots, etc. and we just write that this way.

03:01.090 --> 03:07.270
When you see the radical sign with nothing over here on the left hand side, it means square root.

03:07.420 --> 03:12.970
If we want to take the cube root, we put a little three next to the square root or inside this little

03:12.970 --> 03:14.830
v part of the square root sign.

03:14.830 --> 03:16.690
And we call this the cube root.

03:16.810 --> 03:24.240
This would be the fourth root, this would be the fifth root, etc. So if you ever saw this here with

03:24.240 --> 03:26.410
the two, that would mean the square root.

03:26.410 --> 03:31.960
But because this is the root that we use the most often we don't write this to, it's redundant.

03:31.960 --> 03:33.580
This just means square root.

03:33.580 --> 03:35.620
Even though we don't write the two.

03:35.740 --> 03:41.350
We also want to realize that if we say we're taking the square root of x cubed root of x fourth root

03:41.350 --> 03:47.200
of x fifth root of x, that this is the same as saying x to the one half power.

03:47.200 --> 03:50.860
So this is like converting between radicals and exponents.

03:51.220 --> 03:53.470
These two things are equal to each other.

03:53.500 --> 03:57.610
This is like saying X to the one third power.

03:57.610 --> 04:03.370
In other words, raising X to the exponent or power of one third is the same as taking the third root

04:03.370 --> 04:05.230
or the cube root of x.

04:05.230 --> 04:10.180
So this is x to the one fourth and this is x.

04:11.080 --> 04:12.400
To the one fifth.

04:12.430 --> 04:16.930
Now, one thing you might be wondering about square roots at this point, given these two examples we

04:16.930 --> 04:21.880
have over here, that the square root of four is two, that the square root of nine is three is that

04:21.880 --> 04:25.360
negative numbers would also satisfy these equations.

04:25.360 --> 04:31.030
For instance, you might think that the square root of four could also be negative two, not just positive

04:31.060 --> 04:38.140
two, because negative two times negative two is also equal to four.

04:38.170 --> 04:41.890
So isn't the square root of four also equal to negative two?

04:41.920 --> 04:46.840
Well, in a sense, yes, because negative two times negative two is equal to four.

04:46.870 --> 04:50.380
But and we'll go into more detail about this in algebra.

04:50.470 --> 04:57.520
When we say the square root of four, we agree that we're taking the positive root and ignoring for

04:57.520 --> 04:58.690
now the negative root.

04:58.690 --> 05:04.570
So when you see square root of four, you don't have to consider negative two as a possible square root

05:04.570 --> 05:05.470
of four.

05:05.500 --> 05:07.810
You only have to consider the positive value.

05:07.810 --> 05:11.290
We call the positive value here the principal.

05:11.980 --> 05:12.670
Root.

05:12.940 --> 05:17.300
So the principle root is the positive root, and that's the only one we ever consider.

05:17.320 --> 05:21.430
If you're just given square root of four, you can say the principle root is two, the square root of

05:21.430 --> 05:23.050
four is two.

05:23.080 --> 05:28.060
Or over here, if you're given the square root of nine, the answer to that is three, because we only

05:28.060 --> 05:30.640
have to consider four now the positive value.

05:30.910 --> 05:36.040
And also we want to say that the square root of zero, we always have to think about zero.

05:36.040 --> 05:38.080
It kind of always gets special treatment.

05:38.110 --> 05:41.050
The square root of zero is zero.

05:41.260 --> 05:45.370
Remember, that's the same thing as saying zero to the one half power.

05:45.370 --> 05:48.220
So of course that's equal to zero as well.

05:48.220 --> 05:54.400
So whenever you're taking the square root of some zero value, that's always going to be equal to zero.

05:54.580 --> 06:00.760
And then finally, what do we do if we have something like this, the square root of negative nine instead

06:00.760 --> 06:02.740
of the square root of positive nine?

06:02.950 --> 06:08.680
Well, remember when we talked about sine numbers that there are no two values we can multiply together

06:08.710 --> 06:12.130
that will give us a negative number when those values are the same.

06:12.130 --> 06:12.610
Right?

06:12.760 --> 06:20.470
Our only options here since the values of a square root have to be the same are three times three and

06:20.470 --> 06:23.860
negative three times negative three.

06:24.310 --> 06:26.800
And neither of these is going to give us negative nine.

06:26.800 --> 06:32.080
Three times three is going to be nine and negative three times negative three is going to be nine.

06:32.080 --> 06:38.230
When we multiply two values with the same sign, the result is always going to come out positive, never

06:38.230 --> 06:39.010
negative.

06:39.100 --> 06:44.110
So for now, we're going to say that we can't take the square root of a negative number.

06:44.110 --> 06:49.510
Later on in algebra, we're going to talk about imaginary numbers and how to deal with the square root

06:49.510 --> 06:50.590
of a negative number.

06:50.590 --> 06:56.260
But for now, let's just say that we cannot evaluate the square root of any negative number.

06:56.260 --> 06:58.690
We can only take square root of positive numbers.

06:58.690 --> 07:02.530
And the result there will always be positive or the square root of zero.

07:02.530 --> 07:05.590
And the result there will always be zero.
